/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:2598:      "docstring": "Simulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:2693:        "pipe",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:6119:      "docstring": "Build a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.\n- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.\n\nRule:\n- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.\n- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.\n- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.\n\nKey insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:9304:      "docstring": "Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:9307:        "summary": "Yellow pixels inside colored-border regions move in the direction indicated by the red pixel in the legend with matching background color, stopping at region boundary.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:9315:        "symbol_instructions": "legend on grid edge with background color C and red pixel indicates: yellow pixels in regions with border color C move in direction from blue center to red pixel",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11755:        "summary": "Solid 3x3 colored blocks connected to teal pipelines are split into hollow cross (stays) and solid four-star (moves to pipeline's other end); incomplete shapes at pipeline ends are completed with their counterpart.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11757:        "src": "solid 3x3 colored block at one end of a teal pipeline s",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11758:        "dst": "the other end of the same teal pipeline d",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11760:        "path": "follow teal pipeline from s to reach opposite endpoint d",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11767:          "pipeline tracing",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11772:        "src2": "hollow cross or solid four-star already at one end of teal pipeline s",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11773:        "dst2": "the other end of the same teal pipeline d",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11775:        "path2": "follow teal pipeline from s to reach opposite endpoint d",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11875:        "pipeline",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:11876:        "pipelines",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:16858:      "docstring": "Repair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/code/arc_reference_index.json:35062:      "docstring": "Mark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.",
